course: dictionary
lesson: 13441
title: Hosanna, a shout of praise
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to one special place. Hosanna comes from church and the Bible, so you may not hear it in everyday talk. Read the words around it to find its meaning.
explain: «The crowds waved palm branches and shouted, "Hosanna!"» People who wave branches and shout are cheering and praising. So hosanna is a shout of praise or joy.
explain: Hosanna can also be a noun, a shout or song of praise: «The choir sang a joyful hosanna.» Singing gives the word a second form. Hosanna is first a shout, and it is often sung in hymns.
fact: «Hosanna» is a shout of praise to God.
fact: The crowds shouted «Hosanna!» when Jesus rode into Jerusalem on Palm Sunday.
fact: As a noun, a hosanna is a shout or song of praise or joy.
fact: In Hebrew, the first meaning of hosanna was «save us, please».
fact: Hosanna is sung in many church services and hymns.
exercise: «The crowds waved palm branches and shouted, "Hosanna!"» What does the shout show? | Hunger for lunch | Fear of the dark | Anger at the rain | *Praise and joy
exercise: «The choir sang a joyful hosanna.» What is a hosanna here? | A type of bread | A loud argument | A cold wind | *A song of praise
exercise: What did hosanna first mean in Hebrew? | "Good morning, friend" | "Sit down quietly" | *"Save us, please" | "Goodbye until tomorrow"
test: Which word is a shout or song of praise? | Sunday | Jerusalem | *Hosanna | Palm
test: Where do the crowds shout hosanna in the Palm Sunday story? | At a football match | In a school library | *When Jesus rode into Jerusalem | On a train to Glasgow
exam: What is the best meaning of hosanna? | Anger | *A shout or song of praise | Silence | Hunger
